 are the control group  these plants have been grown without the  mycorrhizae fungi in their roots and the  plants labeled with these are plants  that have been grown with the  mycorrhizal fungi in the roots so the  conclusion is pretty straightforward the  conclusion is that plants and crops tend  to grow better with this mycorrhizae  fungi and this is something that  agriculturalists  and farmers are definitely taking notice  and trying to incorporate into their  growing of crops  so here's a nice little cute little  poster I found for why do you why do  your plants need mycorrhizae fungi and  if you just read through it if the  picture is a little blurry I hope not  mycorrhizal fungi are beneficial and  they form a symbiotic relationship with  plant roots the fungi receives sugar  from the roots in exchange it absorbs  and sends soil nutrients and water back  to the plant so it's a great example of  a mutualistic relationship  when we look at bread molds here's a  here's a bread mold with the fruiting  body above ground the name of the spore  producing structures we never said this  earlier but the name of the spore  producing structure of bread molds is  called the sporangia so make sure you  know that if you're in my biology class  if I were to ask you on a test what do  we call the spore producing structure of  bread molds I hope you would say  sporangia or if I were to ask you which  fungi produced their spores inside of  objects called sporangia I hope you  would say the answer would be bread  molds  when we look at the club fungi here's  three common examples of club fungi  mushrooms puff balls and shelf fun and  the reason they're called Club fungi if  you use your imagination the the  fruiting body often resembles a club  shape some often will describe it it's  kind of like an umbrella shape so if you  look at our most famous cartoon  character that always carried a club  here is bamm-bamm rubble from the  Flintstones he always carried a club  with him we